WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

s1-1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.656
Every shift, you pump solvent, move pallets and slit rolls.

s1-2
00:00:05.656 --> 00:00:10.456
You know these jobs. But today, something might be different.

s1-3
00:00:10.456 --> 00:00:13.280
Before your hands move, take two minutes.

s2-1
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:19.732
The 2-minute rule: pause at the point of work, before you start,

s2-2
00:00:19.732 --> 00:00:24.562
and after any break, handover, interruption, or change.

s2-3
00:00:24.562 --> 00:00:28.297
Two minutes is a guide. What matters is the pause.

s3-1
00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:32.218
You already do line clearance for product.

s3-2
00:00:32.868 --> 00:00:35.874
This is line clearance for people.

s3-3
00:00:35.874 --> 00:00:42.198
It adds to procedures, permits, lockout and guards. It never replaces them.

s4-1
00:00:42.888 --> 00:00:45.226
Stop. Hands off.

s4-2
00:00:45.226 --> 00:00:48.659
Look around the job and compare with the plan.

s4-3
00:00:48.659 --> 00:00:52.916
Ask four questions: What's different? What could hurt us?

s4-4
00:00:52.916 --> 00:00:56.366
Checked or assumed? What if it goes wrong?

s4-5
00:00:56.366 --> 00:01:02.199
Checked means you saw or tested it yourself.
Assumed means you're guessing.

s4-6
00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:08.027
Decide: Go, Fix it, or Stop &amp; get help. That's your call.

s4-7
00:01:08.677 --> 00:01:14.393
Fix it only if you're trained and authorized, and it's a step in your procedure.

s4-8
00:01:14.393 --> 00:01:21.094
No permit yet? Someone else's unfinished work?
Not authorized? Stop &amp; get help.

s4-9
00:01:21.094 --> 00:01:25.182
Not sure, or the fix doesn't work? Stop &amp; get help.

s5-1
00:01:27.132 --> 00:01:31.732
Before you start, you'll move a laminate roll with the roll lifter.

s5-2
00:01:31.732 --> 00:01:35.924
What's different? Nothing. What could hurt us? A heavy roll.

s5-3
00:01:35.924 --> 00:01:39.876
Checked or assumed? Checked. You tested the grip.

s5-4
00:01:39.876 --> 00:01:44.273
What if it goes wrong? Nobody's in the aisle. Your call?

s5-5
00:01:46.423 --> 00:01:48.498
Go. That took seconds.

s6-1
00:01:49.248 --> 00:01:51.670
Most pauses end in Go.

s6-2
00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:56.187
But sometimes a pause catches a problem. That's a save.

s6-3
00:01:56.187 --> 00:01:59.837
Here are four examples, not real events.

s6-4
00:01:59.837 --> 00:02:04.534
Each one asks you twice: find the problem, then pick your call.

s7-1
00:02:06.884 --> 00:02:12.697
In the mix room, before you start, you'll pump flammable solvent from a drum.

s7-2
00:02:12.697 --> 00:02:15.467
Hands off. What would make you stop here?

s7-3
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:24.583
Checked or assumed?
The bonding clamps that stop static sparks are still on their hook.

s7-4
00:02:24.583 --> 00:02:30.081
An unbonded drum or pump can hold a static charge and spark.

s7-5
00:02:30.081 --> 00:02:31.250
Your call?

s7-6
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:39.459
Fix it. Bonding is a step in your procedure. Clamps first, on bare metal.

s7-7
00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:44.226
With drum and pump bonded, the lamp turns green. Go.

s7-8
00:02:44.226 --> 00:02:47.292
No static spark where the vapor comes out.

s8-1
00:02:49.382 --> 00:02:53.994
After your break, you head to roll staging for the next roll.

s8-2
00:02:53.994 --> 00:02:57.052
Look closely. What would make you stop here?

s8-3
00:02:59.802 --> 00:03:04.428
What's different? The roll's up high now. That's a forklift job.

s8-4
00:03:04.428 --> 00:03:10.721
Your card says pallet truck only.
A forklift's a different truck, with its own training.

s8-5
00:03:10.721 --> 00:03:16.330
A co-worker means well: “Line's waiting. Just grab the forklift!”

s8-6
00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:17.657
So, your call?

s8-7
00:03:19.807 --> 00:03:24.488
Stop &amp; get help. You're not authorized to drive a forklift.

s8-8
00:03:24.488 --> 00:03:30.014
Say it kindly: “Thanks, but I'm not trained on that one. I'll call our lead.”

s8-9
00:03:30.014 --> 00:03:33.642
Your lead sends a trained driver. You stand clear.

s8-10
00:03:35.392 --> 00:03:37.134
Then it's Go.

s8-11
00:03:37.134 --> 00:03:40.366
Nobody drives a truck they're not trained on.

s9-1
00:03:42.456 --> 00:03:47.077
At handover, the die cutter's note says it's ready to run.

s9-2
00:03:47.077 --> 00:03:49.429
Now, what would make you stop here?

s9-3
00:03:52.179 --> 00:03:57.919
Checked or assumed? The guard's off the die station, where patches are cut.

s9-4
00:03:57.919 --> 00:04:01.017
And someone else's lock is still on.

s9-5
00:04:01.017 --> 00:04:02.373
What's your call?

s9-6
00:04:04.523 --> 00:04:08.866
Stop &amp; get help. It's someone else's unfinished work.

s9-7
00:04:08.866 --> 00:04:16.600
Only the lock's owner removes it, or a supervisor, using the site's lock-removal procedure.

s9-8
00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:23.969
The owner finishes, puts the guard back and removes their lock.
You run restart checks. Go.

s9-9
00:04:23.969 --> 00:04:28.834
The die cutter never starts with the guard off and the work unfinished.

s10-1
00:04:30.924 --> 00:04:36.588
The slitter changes over to a potent product, strong in tiny amounts.

s10-2
00:04:36.588 --> 00:04:39.263
Your turn. What would make you stop here?

s10-3
00:04:42.013 --> 00:04:49.306
What could hurt us? This product, on bare wrists.
Now it's double gloves and sleeve covers.

s10-4
00:04:49.306 --> 00:04:50.560
And your call?

s10-5
00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:57.268
Fix it. Putting on the right gear is your own gowning step.

s10-6
00:04:57.268 --> 00:05:03.292
Do a normal stop, step away,
and take off your gloves as your procedure says.

s10-7
00:05:03.292 --> 00:05:08.421
Wash hands and wrists with soap and water, not hand sanitizer.

s10-8
00:05:08.421 --> 00:05:14.658
Then put on inner gloves, sleeve covers, and outer gloves over the cuffs. Go.

s10-9
00:05:14.658 --> 00:05:19.761
No small, repeated doses from a product made to pass through skin.

s11-1
00:05:21.251 --> 00:05:27.360
Two were Fix it, two were Stop &amp; get help. All four end in Go once it's safe.

s11-2
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.250
Most pauses end in Go, right away.

s12-1
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:39.505
Anyone can stop a job, staff or contractor, even on a late batch. No punishment, no blame.

s12-2
00:05:40.455 --> 00:05:46.759
Leaders commit to come quickly, thank you, fix the cause and tell you what changed.

s12-3
00:05:46.759 --> 00:05:51.280
The pause isn't a form. Report what you caught the usual way.

s13-1
00:05:52.830 --> 00:06:00.014
Pause before you start, and after any break, handover, interruption or change. Stop and look.

s13-2
00:06:00.014 --> 00:06:02.906
Ask: What's different? What could hurt us?

s13-3
00:06:02.906 --> 00:06:06.357
Checked or assumed? What if it goes wrong?

s13-4
00:06:06.357 --> 00:06:11.124
Decide: Go, Fix it, or Stop &amp; get help.

s13-5
00:06:11.124 --> 00:06:17.200
Fix it only if you're trained and authorized, and it's a step in your procedure.

s13-6
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.760
When in doubt, get help. That's the 2-minute rule: line clearance for people.

s13-7
00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.481
Find out who you call for help: days, nights and weekends.

s13-8
00:06:32.831 --> 00:06:38.284
Follow your site's procedures. This video is awareness training only.
